d":"ENSG00000242247","module":"blue","kme":0.128413,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.137528,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Mic","gene_id":"ENSG00000242247","module":"blue","kme":0.154433,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.625991,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000242247","entrez_id":["26286"],"uniprot":["Q9NP61"],"ncbi_summary":"The protein encoded by this gene is a GTPase-activating protein (GAP) that associates with the Golgi apparatus and regulates the early secretory pathway of proteins. The encoded protein promotes hydrolysis of ADP-ribosylation factor 1 (ARF1)-bound GTP, which is required for the dissociation of coat proteins from Golgi-derived membranes and vesicles. Dissociation of the coat proteins is a prerequisite for the fusion of these vesicles with target compartments. The activity of this protein is sensitive to phospholipids. Multiple transcript variants encoding different isoforms have been found for this gene. This gene was originally known as ARFGAP1, but that is now the name of a related but different gene. [provided by RefSeq, Nov 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q9NP61","text":"GTPase-activating protein (GAP) for ADP ribosylation factor 1 (ARF1), which is essential for COPI vesicle formation and retrograde transport between the Golgi and the endoplasmic reticulum (PubMed:11172815, PubMed:20858901). Hydrolysis of ARF1-bound GTP leads to dissociation of coatomer from Golgi-derived membranes to allow fusion with target membranes (PubMed:11172815, PubMed:20858901). Promotes the proper assembly of the COPI coat lattice, rather than just acting as a simple curvature-dependent GAP like ARFGAP1 (PubMed:20858901)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}